OS Options: show

is usually good here although not required by any means.

> This gets me to the Milo prompt.  Now, I am not sure where to go from here.  

If you are booting from CD which was created specifically for booting
with milo, which means that it a has a file like milo.cfg or
etc/milo.conf, try typing

  b scd0::

(or whatever your CD device happens to be) and this should present
you with a list of labels to choose from.  Options in milo.cfg should
be set up correctly for that disk.

> What is the correct command to 
> get me to the next step?

Assuming that you have some "recent" milo, where "recent" means not
older that some three years or so, then it should be supporting initrd
and then a full boot command may be something of that sort:

  b sr0:/kernels/vlmilo.gz initrd=/images/cdrom.img

but details depend on what you have on that CD and that is why
relying on "local" milo configuration files is such good idea.
If this is Debian or any other distro is relevant only to an extent
if somebody packaging things bothered to create milo.cfg.

Kernels compiled with LEGACY_LOAD_ADDRESS not set, to behave correctly
on machines with "big" SRM consoles, as a rule will not load with milos.

   Michal


