> I'm running kernel 2.6.0-test5 now, but behaviour was the same with kernel
> 2.4.20 and older. I tried ntpd from version 4.0.?? to 4.1.2. There are no
> IDE discs in the system, but the problem still might be disc i/o related.
> 
> Has anybody else seen this?

That sounds like timer interrupts are getting dropped.
Being as this is a test release of a very early kernel, one
guess would be that perhaps interrupts are left disabled too
long somewhere in the kernel.  Or, perhaps there's a
different bug with similar symptoms.

As I read your first paragraph, I immediately thought of my
experience with IDE after my Alpha died and I was forced to
downgrade to X86.  By default, DMA and/or other
optimizations were disabled for the CDROM drives, which
caused the clock to run 3-4X too slow.  (Tuning some hdparm
options have solved that problem.)

One interesting test would be to compare CPU-bound
vs. disk-bound loads to see which one (or both) cause the
drift you're seeing.

Good luck.

