Keith Harwood <vitalmis@bigpond.net.au> wrote:
> 
> I have had a similar problem, but persistent. Using 2.2.14
> and 2.4.20 the system clock runs slow all the time. It
> doesn't seem to be related to load. The hardware clock does
> much better. All the books say the system clock is better
> than the hardware clock, so there is something wrong. I
> haven't ben able to find out what is happening. Anyone got
> any ideas?

There is a clock calibration routine in time.c in alpha specific
parts of a kernel code.  It goes like that "if a measured frequency
is too wildly off from an expected value then assume that this
measurement is botched and use a frequency from a table".
The problem is that this "wildly off" limits are set too crude
and on various occasions were rejecting for me good values
and as a result my clock was getting differences of few seconds
per hour.  Changing that function not to be so quick on rejects
resulted in a very good system clock without an external reference.

   Michal


